.Dd October 9, 2026
.Dt DOORFS 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm doorfs
.Nd kernel implementation of Doors IPC
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "options DOORFS"
.Cd "options DOORCTL"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
implements process-local Door creation, descriptor capabilities, synchronous
and asynchronous invocation, server worker binding, descriptor transfer, and
persistent shared-memory regions.
The public interface is provided by
.Xr door 3
and transported through
.Xr door 2 .
.Pp
Door descriptors have file type
.Dv DTYPE_DOOR .
They are not byte streams: ordinary read, write, truncate, polling, and vnode
I/O operations are rejected.
Descriptors may be duplicated, inherited, passed between processes, restricted
with Capsicum, and published through
.Xr namefs 4 .
.Pp
A descriptor pins one Door generation.
Revocation is visible to every duplicate and aborts operations according to the
call and cancellation policy.
Closing one duplicate does not invalidate the others.
Owner exit revokes owned Doors, wakes workers and clients, and removes the owner
anchor while existing references complete their normal teardown.
.Ss Invocation modes
Ordinary Doors use synchronous request/reply calls.
.Dv DOOR_ONEWAY
snapshots the request before returning to the caller and supplies no reply.
.Dv DOOR_NO_RETURN
is a compatible empty-reply hint and preserves synchronous semantics.
The current empty-reply fast path does not depend on this attribute.
Descriptor MOVE operations are transactional and are not committed when input
validation or dispatch fails.
.Ss Data transfer
Small data uses copied kernel buffers.
Large synchronous requests and replies use held pages at direction-specific
thresholds. Ordinary ONEWAY and kernel async request bytes are copied
snapshots; persistent SHM is a separate shared-memory path.
Per-Door data and descriptor limits are available through
.Xr door_getparam 3
and
.Xr door_setparam 3 .
.Ss Persistent shared memory
Persistent SHM regions are owned by a Door generation.
The visible size is the exact requested byte length; VM page rounding is an
implementation detail and bytes in the rounded tail are not valid data.
Every reference is checked for integer overflow and containment within its
region.
Regions belonging to different clients cannot be substituted across Door
generations.
.Pp
The checked SPSC ring API stores offsets, sequence numbers, and committed
lengths rather than process-local pointers.
Reserve/commit and acquire/consume enforce one outstanding producer reservation
and one outstanding consumer view per handle.
Only the committed prefix of a reused slot is exposed.
.Ss Jail and capability policy
Door creation records the owner's prison.
New capabilities may be acquired only from the same prison unless the host
created the endpoint with
.Dv DOOR_CROSS_JAIL .
The flag may be used only by prison 0.
Once acquired, a descriptor remains a capability and calls do not repeat the
jail check.
.Pp
Capsicum rights separate call, service, administrative, information, and SHM
operations.
.Ss Optional named registry
.Cd "options DOORCTL"
adds
.Pa /dev/doorctl
and a logical named-service registry.
It is not required for direct descriptors, the Door syscall, persistent SHM,
or namefs publication.
If omitted, the named functions in
.Xr door 3
fail when opening
.Pa /dev/doorctl .
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The read-only resource ceilings are exposed below
.Va kern.doorfs ,
including per-process Doors, per-Door servers, SHM regions, and SHM bytes.
When
.Cd "options DOORCTL"
is present, registry limits and counts are exposed below
.Va kern.doorctl .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr door 2 ,
.Xr door 3 ,
.Xr namefs 4 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr jail 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in this FreeBSD Doors implementation.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Shared memory is not a trust boundary between processes that are deliberately
given access to the same writable region.
The kernel guarantees mapping and reference boundaries, page-rounded isolation,
generation ownership, and access permissions; applications must still define
their own message validation and producer/consumer synchronization.
